":"completed","target":"CHAD Bash"},{"accepted_at":"2026-11-28T06:03:27.131670+00:00","exit_code":0,"finished_at":"2026-11-28T06:03:30.688862+00:00","id":"a737331378da050ff265","operation":"run_bash","outcome":"completed","target":"CHAD Bash"}],"repository_evidence":[{"blob_sha":"89703118ae40aa1863197655f0a80857d64ed045","committed_at":"2026-11-28T05:55:09+00:00","end_line":1350,"excerpt":"## 36. Why stabilization does not enlarge the torsion budget\n\nThe proof uses two types of rank. The analytic representation may need a very large matrix rank, and block replication increases it further. The geometric torsion theorem must instead use a rank bounded explicitly in terms of degree. Integral homological stability reconciles them.\n\nThe source KStability asserts that the block inclusion $GL_n(R)\\to GL_{n+1}(R)$ induces an integral homology isomorphism in degree $i$ for $n\\ge2i+s+2$, where $s$ bounds Bass stable rank. The ring of integers has stable rank at most two: in a unimodular row choose a nonzero coordinate, use the Chinese remainder theorem over its finitely many maximal prime divisors to modify another coordinate by a multiple of the last, then delete the last. No maximal ideal contains the resulting row. The case with all earlier coordinates zero is immediate.\n\nThere is also independent classical support: van der Kallen, [Homology Stability for Linear Groups](https://webspace.science.uu.nl/~kalle101/homstab1980.pdf), Inventiones Mathematicae 60 (1980), pp. 269\u2013295, convention 2.2 and Theorem 4.11 on p. 289, gives an even stronger range for integral constant coefficients. The source's mathematical stability contract is therefore established by classical literature, without asserting replay of the submitted proof.\n\n","id":"9b76baeadfc2e5cf3d02","observed_at":"2026-11-28T06:03:08.583913+00:00","path":"docs/domains/rh_a26_audit_explained.md","policy":"GCL_PUBLIC_REPOSITORY_EVIDENCE/1","redactions":{},"repository":"grandchallenge/MATH-PROGRAMME","revision":"aca6bce4e83e4d919f2bda41c277b447efd10faa","source_sha256":"3e5e200c183a211763db2c27f415630efe065227159b295baa507da5f3431d9e","start_line":1343,"total_lines":1436},{"blob_sha":"4335179546940bec81b177f0e8c4a974baa86fd5","committed_at":"2026-11-28T05:32:53+00:00","end_line":1317,"excerpt":"## 34. Why geometry can constrain integral torsion\n\nThe earlier rank argument cannot see finite-order classes. Here the source changes tools. An exact augmented equivariant chain complex built from geometric simplices has permutation modules $P_p=\\mathbb Z[X_p]$. Let $A$ bound their orbit counts, $s$ the stabilizer orders, and $b$ the final boundary column's integral one-norm. These are separate sources of arithmetic complexity.\n\nTaking coinvariants identifies generators in each orbit. The final relations still have one-norm at most $b$. Choose independent relation columns and complete them by unit vectors. The index of the resulting full-rank lattice is the absolute determinant, at most $b^A$. The saturation quotient representing torsion is bounded by this index, even for a rank-deficient relation module.\n\nFor a transitive orbit with stabilizer $H$, Shapiro's lemma identifies its permutation-module homology with the homology of $H$. In positive degree $j$, the latter is killed by $|H|$ and is generated by at most $|H|^j$ elements. With at most $A$ orbits, its order is at most $s^{As^j}$. The long exact sequence of homology shifts one degree at a time, multiplying these costs. Thus\n\n$$\n|\\mathrm{Tor}\\, H_m(G,\\mathbb Z)|\\le b^A s^{A\\sum_{j=1}^{m}s^j}\\le b^A s^{mAs^m}.\n$$\n\nFor ordinary face deletion $b=m+2$. This is the general theorem behind KH.mainFace. Exactness, finite orbit counts and finite stabilizers are indispensable; a mere quotient cell count would not suffice.\n\n![The determinant cost and positive-degree stabilizer costs combine in the homology bound](../assets/rh-a26-geometric-torsion.svg)\n\n## 35. From number-field lattices to finite orbit counts\n\n","id":"4a9465ca6848107bba09","observed_at":"2026-11-28T06:04:22.830841+00:00","path":"docs/domains/rh_a26_audit_explained.md","policy":"GCL_PUBLIC_REPOSITORY_EVIDENCE/1","redactions":{},"repository":"grandchallenge/MATH-PROGRAMME","revision":"34cac3e18d695ea7bb65cb3c5c02f263c4c35eee","source_sha256":"d520a682e3cc5b4b3e1fbc805463c4b4c735ece32156971e498289ad75064f33","start_line":1300,"total_lines":1418},{"blob_sha":"055173ed41979686ee20f025f6a26ba37bb9dc43","committed_at":"2026-11-28T00:55:45+00:00","end_line":53,"excerpt":"\"\"\"Exact finite replay: joint fixed-witness elimination and atom-closure transfer.\"\"\"\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef dot(a,b): return (a&b).bit_count()&1\n\ndef basis(xs):\n    piv={}\n    for x in xs:\n        while x:\n            i=x.bit_length()-1\n            if i not in piv: piv[i]=x; break\n            x^=piv[i]\n    return tuple(piv.values())\n\ndef space(bs):\n    s={0}\n    for b in bs:s|={a^b for a in s}\n    return tuple(sorted(s))\n\ndef coordinates(n):return tuple(sum(((p>>i)&1)<<p for p in range(1<<n)) for i in range(n))\n\ndef filtration(n,V):\n    F=[((1<<(1<<n))-1,)]\n    for _ in range(n):F.append(basis([*F[-1],*(a&b for a in F[-1] for b in V)]))\n    assert len(F[-1])==1<<n\n    return F\n\ndef level(F,l):return next(d for d,b in enumerate(F) if any(dot(l,a) for a in b))\n\ndef active(n,V,L):\n    F=filtration(n,V);live=[(l,level(F,l)) for l in L if not any(dot(l,v) for v in V)]\n    P=sum((1<<(d-1))-1 for l,d in live)\n    forms=space(V);freq=Counter(u&v for u in forms for v in forms)\n    scores={};totals={l:0 for l,d in live}\n    for g,m in freq.items():\n        saving=0\n        for l,d in live:\n            if any(dot(l,g&w) for w in F[d-2]):\n                totals[l]+=m;saving+=1<<(d-2)\n        scores[g]=saving\n    den=len(forms)**2\n    assert all(8*total>=3*den for total in totals.values())\n    assert 16*sum(freq[g]*s for g,s in scores.items())>=3*den*P\n    g=max(scores,key=scores.get);W=basis([*V,g]);F1=filtration(n,W)\n    assert P-scores[g]==sum((1<<(level(F1,l)-1))-1 for l in L)\n    return W,len(live),P,len(freq),len(live)*len(freq)\n\ndef target_trials():\n    steps=checks=cases=0\n    for n in (2,3):\n        M=(1<<(1<<n))-1;V0=basis([M,*coordinates(n)])\n        all_live=[l for l in range(1,M+1) if all(not dot(l,v) for v in V0)]\n        for f in range(M+1):\n","id":"edeff181f69a20ff9698","observed_at":"2026-11-28T06:03:13.698669+00:00","path":"research/SCC-001/joint_elimination_replay.py","policy":"GCL_PUBLIC_REPOSITORY_EVIDENCE/1","redactions":{},"repository":"grandchallenge/MATHSOLVE","revision":"73d1572d981e82c1e1f41d02704700bee0bcd24b","source_sha256":"b69d329227c206d3b0fa5756f8243c1be9f7289d4c287f4d636b749d4a7987a5","start_line":1,"total_lines":93}],"retention_limit":48,"schema":"GCL_CONTROL_ACTIVITY_FEED/2","withheld_candidates":0}
